<title>BELGIUM: Belgian UGB says faces bankruptcy if no union deal.</title>
<headline>Belgian UGB says faces bankruptcy if no union deal.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-01-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Belgian steelmaker Usines Gustave Boel SA, which is negotiating an alliance with Dutch Hoogovens, said on Friday it faces bankruptcy if it cannot reach a labour agreement soon with unions.</p>
<p>&quot;There has to be an agreement in the coming days. It's that or the company Usines Gustave Boel filing for bankruptcy with the loss of 1,200 jobs,&quot; UGB spokesman Pierre Stievenart told Reuters.</p>
<p>Hoogovens has said it will take a 50 percent stake and invest three billion Belgian francs in UGB if there is an accord to restructure the company, which is family owned.</p>
<p>UGB has said around eight hundred jobs would be kept under the deal, which was rejected by unions in December.</p>
<p>&quot;We are ready to begin negotiating tonight or even tomorrow (Saturday). There's no time to lose,&quot; Stievenart said.</p>
<p>He said the company had until the end of January to accept Hoogovens offer. &quot;We are talking about 10 days,&quot; he said.</p>
